Harry Shapiro writes:
> 
> Perry has indicated that jamesdon@jamesdon.infoserv.com has been posting
> forged mail to the list.
> 
> Ray is going to check this out and if this is the case suspend Mr
> Donaldson's list usage. 
  
   I have examined the raw headers in the message queue and Donald is
indeed guilty of pseudospoofing. The list software does not rewrite
From: lines so it's impossible that a bug caused it. 

   It appears that Donald did not intend to actually pseudospoof Perry, but
to avoid the list security as he included his own sig and left the
Message-Id and From envelope as normal.

   The list software can not prevent spoofing as there is no way, using
header data alone, to authenticate a message. However, from the very beginning
the list software has had another method of preventing individuals from being
spoofed but it is turned off as default.

   Basically, you can elect to have a password associated with your
list settings. When the password is non-null, the list will prevent
any posts or commands from being executed on that e-mail address unless a
"::password <your password>" is supplied as the first list command at the
beginning of every post.

   It's a hassle to use, but if you have a mailer which can automatically
append "::pass foobar" and ":::" at the beginning of every message then it
might be useful.

   This is simpler than using PGP signatures, easily checked with little
CPU (MD5/crypt()) and protects you from any spoofing. I can also hack the
list software to add a notice to the headers of messages which were
password verified to let others know that the message is semi-authentic.

-Ray


  
