Date: Sat, 13 Nov 93 11:58:45 PST
From: pierre@shell.portal.com (Pierre Uszynski)
Subject: META: money, money

Just a few points about list charges:

- There was a poll on list charges done by the board. So
at least they must have received some input from people
opposed to the idea. They at least got MY opposing
message.

- I did suggest an alternate solution to running lists
off one site (resulting in high load and bandwidth). It
was rejected (I think a bit superficially) by the
"management". On the other hand, I'm not interested in
starting a parallel free list using that method.

- It will take some time before the idea of paying for
Internet services such as mailing lists (as opposed to
access) are fully accepted. Everybody is used to them
being free. Some people are net even used to the idea of
paying for access yet.

- Even when people get used to the idea of paying for
services (even as minimal as list management), it is
not clear what constitutes a reasonnable price. Again it
will take experimentation before prices statbilize (access
prices have not stabilized yet). Comparisons to the
value of the time spend reading the list are certainly
valid (but do you compare to movies, hot tubs, newspapers,
scientific journals, books, salaries, or netnews???) As
far as the amount of junk mail goes, it's closer to netnews,
IMHO.

- Time-based subscriptions versus management decisions
resulting in suspension, or removal, from the list may
cause legal conflicts. Just as a magazine cannot
arbitrarily cancel somebody's subscriptions (or can they?).
A caller contract to which subscribers agree could solve that.

- For-money access can also heighten expectations of
reliability and security that the "management" may or may not
be able or willing to support.

- The cost of check collection and cashing, accounting,
caller contract development, and other management load
added by the fact that the list is for-money may well
eat up all income from subscription.

I'm not proposing any ready-made solution. I still believe
there must be solutions for distributing the computing
and bandwidth load of mailing lists, but for these to be
considered the  management would have to release some of
it's "ownership" of the list and that does not seem about
to happen.

I do not know yet whether I will stay in this list
when it activates it's subscription mechanism.

